{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789210735,"nonce":"LVnE0G21NG0P09JV4MrJjG/xQ7E=","title":"JIT direct-path SubscriptSet on known List elided the write barrier","message":"Fixed; see git log for the landing commit. Record migrated from session memory 2026-09-12.\n\n**Symptom:** SIGSEGV at `0x0000000000000004` inside `collect_minor → trace_object` after a few minutes of running the nature-garden demo. Frequency increased once `scatterGrass_` was refactored into a 3-bucket loop that calls `device.createBuffer()` and writes `_grassBuffers[j] = buf` per bucket — every `createBuffer()` is a chance for a minor GC to fire between the young-gen buffer allocation and the next iteration.\n\n**Root cause:** `Instruction::SubscriptSet` on a known-List receiver hits a JIT direct path in `src/codegen/mod.rs:4620-4697` that inlines:\n- bounds check\n- `Str { src: val_reg, mem: Mem::new(elem_addr, 0) }` directly into `list.elements[idx]`\n\nThe runtime fallback `wren_subscript_set` (called via the slow path) emits `gc.write_barrier(source, value)` after the store — but the JIT direct path skipped it. Before tier-up the BC interpreter takes the slow path, so the bug stays dormant. Once the function hits `jit_threshold` and tiers up, every `list[i] = obj` is barrier-free, the old(List) → young(Value) edge never enters `remembered_set`, and the next minor GC drops the young value while the list still holds the (now-stale) pointer.\n\n`trace_object` faults at 0x4 because `*ObjHeader` reads `gc_mark` at offset 4 of a header whose backing memory has been recycled and reads as a small integer that, when treated as a header pointer for the next iteration, is unmapped.\n\n**Companion sites with the same class of bug (all fixed in the same pass):**\n- `core/list.rs:264` — `list_sort` rewrites every slot via `list.set(i, v)` without barrier.\n- `core/list.rs:300` — `list_sort_by` `list.swap(i, min_idx)` without barrier.\n- `core/random.rs:348-349` — `random_shuffle` Fisher-Yates `list.set(i, b); list.set(j, a)` without barriers.\n\n**Fix** (file: `src/codegen/mod.rs:4696-4722`):\n\n```rust\nself.mf.emit(MachInst::Str { src: val_reg, mem: Mem::new(elem_addr, 0) });\n// Write barrier: gated on the value possibly being an object,\n// same predicate the SetField path at line 3422 uses.\nif self.gc_value_reps.get(value.0 as usize).copied()\n   .unwrap_or(GcValueRep::MaybeObject).is_gc_root()\n{\n    self.mf.emit(MachInst::CallRuntime {\n        name: \"wren_write_barrier\",\n        args: vec![recv_reg, val_reg],\n        ret:  Some(dst),\n    });\n} else {\n    self.mf.emit(MachInst::Mov { dst, src: val_reg });\n}\n```\n\n**Related:** [[project-fiber-set-reg-barrier]] — the analogous frame-slot case fixed via unconditional Fiber tracing in `collect_minor` / `collect_major`. The two fixes cover independent leak channels (fiber stack writes vs. list element writes) — do not remove either; the unconditional Fiber sweep does NOT cover other old-gen objects.\n\n**How to apply:**\n- If `trace_object` faults at 0x4 again, suspect a NEW write site missing a barrier — `grep \"list\\.set\\|map\\.entries\\.insert\\|\\.swap\\(\" src/runtime/core/` and verify each call site is followed by `ctx.write_barrier(args[0], value)`.\n- Always rebuild `hatch` + `wlift` after touching codegen/`*.rs` — the runtime + JIT change won't take effect until `cargo build --release` reproduces the binary.\n- The trigger pattern is \"tight loop that allocates young objects and writes them into an old-gen list\" — `_grassBuffers[j] = device.createBuffer()` was the exemplar.","files":null}]}